%S A400195 1,1,1,1,1,2,1,1,1,1,1,2,1,1,2,1,1,3,1,2,1,1,1,2,1,1,1,2,1,4,1,1,1,1,
%T A400195 2,3,1,1,1,2,1,4,1,1,4,1,1,2,1,1,1,1,1,4,1,2,1,1,1,4,1,1,2,1,1,4,1,1,
%U A400195 1,2,1,3,1,1,2,1,2,2,1,2,1,1,1,4,1,1,1,2,1,6,2,1,1,1,1,2,1,1,2,3,1,2,1,2,3
%N A400195 Maximum number of odd terms in a 2-dense sublist of divisors of n.
%C A400195 a(n) is also the largest number in the n-th row of A384225.
%C A400195 Conjecture: a(n) is the ratio between the maximum number of terms in a 2-dense sublist of divisors of n and the ruler function of n.
%H A400195 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A400195/b400195.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A400195 Conjecture: a(n) = A400194(n) / A001511(n).
%e A400195 For n = 70 the divisors of 70 are 1, 2, 5, 7, 10, 14, 35, 70. There are three 2-dense sublists of divisors, they are [1, 2], [5, 7, 10, 14], [35, 70]. The first sublist has only one odd term. The second sublist has two odd terms. The third sublist has only one odd term. Therefore the maximum number of odd terms in a 2-dense sublist is 2, so a(70) = 2.
%t A400195 A400195[n_] := Max[Map[Count[#, _?OddQ] &, Split[Divisors[n], #2 <= 2*# &]]];
%t A400195 Array[A400195, 100] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 04 2026 *)
%Y A400195 Cf. A001511, A237271, A384222, A384225, A400194.
%K A400195 nonn,new
%O A400195 1,6
%A A400195 _Omar E. Pol_, Sep 28 2026
